{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"08","chapter_title":"AI Conversations as a Teacher","title":"Module 4 Dialogue Studio","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting module 4 dialogue studio to a publish checklist and live HTML page, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the publish checklist for the Module 4 dialogue studio shows a defensible decision in CAT 100"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-04","statement":"Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher","cat100_supplement_module_4_dialogue_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/04/07"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Module 4 Dialogue Studio\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Module 4 Dialogue Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/04-08-module-4-dialogue-studio.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Module 4 Dialogue Studio: Module 4 Dialogue Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe studio is where your Module 4 work stops being files on your laptop and becomes a real web page anyone can open by URL. Publishing to GitHub Pages means writing plain HTML, pushing it to a repository, and letting GitHub serve it as a live site. The skill that matters is not making it pretty; it is making it correct, so the page has a real title, real headings, and links that actually reach your evidence files. A page that \"looks fine\" in the browser but has no `<title>` and a dead link fails the moment a reviewer tries to use it.\n\nThis matters because a public page is the most exposed artifact in the course. There is no instructor standing next to it to explain what you meant. The markup has to carry the meaning on its own: the structure tags tell the browser and any assistive technology what each piece is, and the working link is what lets the evidence behind the page be reached. Getting the HTML right is what makes the publication trustworthy.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the publish checklist for the Module 4 dialogue studio shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student's first `index.html` is three lines: an `<html>` tag, the bare text \"my ai project,\" and an empty `<a>link</a>`. It loads without error, so the student assumes it is done. But the browser tab shows the file path instead of a name, because there is no `<title>`. The heading is not a heading, just loose text, so a screen reader announces nothing structural. The link goes nowhere, because the `<a>` has no `href`.\n\nThe page that looks finished is actually broken in every way that counts. A correct version adds a `<title>` in the head, marks the heading with `<h1>`, and gives the link a real `href` pointing to an evidence file. After pushing to GitHub Pages, the student opens the live URL and confirms the title shows in the tab, the heading renders large, and the link downloads the real file.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA publishable page is checked against a small set of structural requirements, each tied to a specific HTML element.\n\n| Requirement | Element | Why it matters |\n|-------------|---------|----------------|\n| Page title | `<title>` inside `<head>` | shows in the browser tab, search results, and bookmarks |\n| Document type | `<!DOCTYPE html>` | tells the browser to use standard rendering |\n| Real heading | `<h1>` | gives the page a structural top level for readers and assistive tech |\n| Working link | `<a href=\"file\">` | actually reaches the evidence; an empty `href` is a dead end |\n\nThe distinction the studio enforces is structure versus appearance. A heading is not \"big text\"; it is an `<h1>` element that means \"this is the main heading,\" which the browser then happens to render large. Using a real `<h1>` instead of bare bold text is what lets a screen reader, a search engine, and a future editor understand the page. This is the [WCAG](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) idea of semantic markup: the tag carries the meaning, not the visual size.\n\nGitHub Pages serves whatever is in the published branch, so \"publishing\" is just pushing your `index.html` and opening the live URL. The verification step is non-negotiable: you load the actual URL, not the local file, because a link that works on your disk can break once the site is served from a different root.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your page links to an evidence file. It opens fine when you double-click index.html on your laptop, but clicking the same link on the live GitHub Pages URL returns a 404. Where is the fix?\" explanation=\"A link that passes locally and 404s live is a path problem: the file was not committed, or it sits in a subfolder the href does not name. The only test that counts is clicking the link on the served site, so you fix the href (or commit the missing file) and push again, not the local copy.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Fix the href or commit the missing file, then re-test on the live URL\", \"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Nothing; if it works locally the page is correctly published\", \"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Add a background color so the page looks more finished\", \"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Replace the link text with bigger bold styling\", \"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe Module 4 studio is where you publish the work as a real web page on GitHub Pages, so the dialogue artifact and its evidence are public and linkable. A freshman writes plain HTML, then checks it against a publish checklist. Follow the steps.\n\nStep 1. A student's first `index.html` looks like this:\n\n```html\n<html>\nmy ai project\n<a>link</a>\n</html>\n```\n\nIt loads, but it has no title, the heading is not a heading, and the link points nowhere.\n\nStep 2. Run the four-item publish checklist and mark each PASS or FAIL:\n\n| Item | Requirement | Draft above |\n|------|-------------|-------------|\n| Page title | `<title>` in the head | FAIL (no head, no title) |\n| Real heading | content uses `<h1>` | FAIL (bare text) |\n| Working link | `<a href=\"...\">` points to a file | FAIL (empty href) |\n| One artifact shown | the dialogue or table is embedded | FAIL (nothing shown) |\n\nStep 3. Rewrite the markup so every item passes:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <title>CAT 100 Module 4: AI Dialogue Studio</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>My Teacher-AI Dialogue Project</h1>\n    <p>This page links my four Module 4 artifacts.</p>\n    <a href=\"02_factcheck_table.xlsx\">Fact-check table (3 claims verified)</a>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nStep 4. Verify the publish. After committing to the published branch (Settings > Pages > Deploy from a branch > main, or a GitHub Actions deploy), you open the live URL once the Pages build finishes (allow a minute or two for the build and cache to update) and confirm the title shows in the browser tab, the heading renders large, and the link downloads the real file. You also log one rejected suggestion. The assistant you drafted with (a current tool such as ChatGPT, Claude, Microsoft Copilot, or Gemini; model and version names churn) offered to open the page with \"Research shows students remember 90% of what they teach others (Dale, 1969).\" You rejected it because that statistic is fabricated and the citation misattributed, the exact claim you debunked in 04.03, and you note your disclosed AI use on the page in line with Chapter 1's responsible-use expectations. The before/after markup plus the passing checklist is the evidence: a reader can view source and see exactly what you fixed.\n\nOrder the steps that take the page from broken markup to a verified live publish:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Add the structure tags: DOCTYPE, a title in the head, and an h1 heading\", \"Give each link a real href that points to a committed evidence file\", \"Commit index.html and every linked file, then push to the published branch\", \"Open the live GitHub Pages URL, not the local file\", \"Verify on the live URL: title in the tab, h1 renders, and every link reaches its file\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nNow run the publish checklist one item at a time, predicting each verdict before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run the publish checklist on the live page\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The first markup is just an html tag, the bare text ’my ai project,’ and an empty link. The browser tab shows a file path instead of a name. Which item failed and what fixes it?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Page title failed: add a title element inside the head, such as ’CAT 100 Module 4: AI Dialogue Studio’\",\"Nothing failed; the page rendered without an error\",\"The heading failed: make the text a larger font\",\"The link failed: change its color\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Page title failed. With no title element the tab shows the file path. Adding a real title in the head makes the page name show in the tab, search results, and bookmarks.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The main heading is just bare text. Why is making it ’big bold text’ NOT the fix?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A heading is an h1 element that carries meaning; size alone tells a screen reader and search engine nothing\",\"Big bold text is the fix; appearance is all that matters\",\"Headings should never be styled\",\"Bare text is already a valid heading\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Use a real h1. The tag carries the structural meaning (this is the main heading) that assistive tech and search engines read; the large rendering is a side effect, not the point. This is the [WCAG](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) idea of semantic markup.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Finally, give the link a real href to a committed file and verify on the live URL, not the local copy. You also reject the assistant’s offer to open the page with the fabricated 90%-retention statistic: confident, fluent advice still has to pass the evidence check before it goes on a public page, and you log the rejection in your trace.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nPublish for correctness first and appearance last, and verify on the live URL, not the local file. A page is ready only when the title, heading, and links pass on the served site.\n\nRun these three checks after you push:\n\n1. **Tab check:** Does the browser tab show your `<title>` text rather than a file path?\n2. **Structure check:** Is the main heading a real `<h1>`, so it carries meaning and not just size?\n3. **Link check:** Does each link, clicked on the live URL, actually reach the evidence file it names?\n\nIf a link works locally but 404s on the live site, the path is wrong relative to the served root. Fix the `href`, not the local copy.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"if it renders, it is correct.\" A page with no `<title>` and bare text headings renders fine to a sighted user with a fast eye, but it fails for search engines, bookmarks, and screen readers. Rendering is the floor, not the standard.\n\nThe second misreading is reaching for styling before structure. Students often ask the AI for colors and fonts while the link is still dead. Decoration on a broken page is wasted effort; the checklist puts a working link above a background color on purpose, because a reachable artifact is the point of publishing.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the publish checklist to a link that breaks only on the live site. Locally, your page links to `02_factcheck_table.xlsx` and it opens, because the file sits next to `index.html` on your disk. You push to GitHub Pages, open the live URL, click the link, and get a 404. The structure and title pass; the link check fails. The cause is almost always a path mismatch: the file was not committed, or it lives in a subfolder the `href` does not name.\n\nYou fix it by committing the file to the same folder the page expects, or by correcting the `href` to the real served path, then push again and re-test on the live URL. The walkthrough makes the verification step concrete: the only test that counts is clicking the link on the served site, because the served root differs from your laptop's folder. A page that passes locally and fails live has not actually been published in a usable state.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **requirement** on the left, **the element that satisfies it** on the right. A strong reader does not say \"the page looks done\"; they say \"the heading passes because it uses `<h1>`, not because the text is large.\" Pointing to the element is what proves the requirement is met.\n\nA strong reader of Module 4 Dialogue Studio can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which element provides the page title, and does it show in the tab?\n2. Is the main heading a real `<h1>`, or just styled text pretending to be one?\n3. Does each link reach its file when clicked on the live URL, not the local copy?\n4. What did you fix for correctness before touching appearance?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reviewer can open the live URL, see the title in the tab, and click a link that reaches a real evidence file, then view source and see correct structural markup. Keep the before and after HTML so the reviewer can see which tags you added and why.\n\nA useful closing note for the artifact would read: \"I added a `<title>` and an `<h1>` because the first version had neither, so the tab showed a file path and screen readers found no heading. I rejected the assistant's suggestion to open the page with the 90%-retention statistic because the figure is fabricated and its citation misattributed, and an unverifiable claim has no place on a public page.\" That ties each fix to a failed requirement and defends one rejected suggestion.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the publishing skill from a course project page to a personal resume site, which CAT 100 also asks you to build. The same correctness checklist governs: a `<title>` so the tab and search results name you, real `<h1>` and `<h2>` headings so the structure is readable by assistive tech and recruiters' tools, and working links to your projects rather than dead ones. But the stakes raise the bar on verification, because a broken link on a resume site is seen by an employer, not a grader. The structure tags and the live-URL test transfer directly; what changes is that the audience now includes people who will not give you a second chance to fix a 404.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how publishing a correct page connects to the chapter’s wider ideas:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\", \"cat100_supplement_module_4_dialogue_studio\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the publish checklist for the Module 4 dialogue studio packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/04-08-module-4-dialogue-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"publish checklist for the Module 4 dialogue studio\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"04.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the publish checklist for the Module 4 dialogue studio.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the publish checklist for the Module 4 dialogue studio for CAT 100 04.08. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_module_4_dialogue_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm on the live URL that the title shows in the tab, the heading is a real `<h1>`, every link reaches its file, and your trace logs one suggestion you rejected with a reason.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- Walter, Y. (2024). Embracing the future of artificial intelligence in the classroom. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education. https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-024-00448-3\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_4_dialogue_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_4_dialogue_studio","cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher"],"stem":"The AI's teaching advice on your published dialogue page reads smoothly and sounds confident, and the page renders fine. Is the advice itself ready to publish?","options":["Yes; fluent, confident advice is accurate advice.","No; fluent advice must still be checked claim by claim against evidence before it is published.","Yes, as long as the page loads.","No, because advice can never appear on a public page."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Fluency is a style property, not an evidence check; each claim in the advice has to be verified before it goes on the public page.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_fluent_equals_correct","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_4_dialogue_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_4_dialogue_studio"],"stem":"Your dialogue page shows the teacher persona block at the top. What role does that persona actually play in the artifact?","options":["It is flavor text that makes the chat sound friendlier.","It is a constraint that fixes the AI's role, audience, and limits, so the dialogue can be checked against it.","It is decoration a reviewer is expected to skip.","It only matters if the page also has custom styling."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The persona is the contract the dialogue must honor; treating it as decoration means the tone, audience, and bounds were never really set.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_persona_is_decoration","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_module_4_dialogue_studio"],"stem":"While drafting your page with a current assistant (ChatGPT, Claude, Copilot, or Gemini), it suggests opening with 'Research shows students remember 90% of what they teach others (Dale, 1969).' It sounds authoritative. What is the strongest move?","options":["Accept it; a citation means the claim is verified.","Reject it and log the rejection, because the statistic is fabricated and the cited author never made the claim.","Accept it but remove the citation so no one can check it.","Keep it because it makes the page look researched."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Confident, fluent advice with a citation still has to pass claim-by-claim verification; this is the fabricated statistic and misattribution debunked in 04.03, so you reject it and record the rejection in your trace.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_fluent_equals_correct","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_module_4_dialogue_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision","cat100_supplement_module_4_dialogue_studio"],"stem":"After pushing your dialogue page to GitHub Pages, what confirms the artifact is actually done?","options":["The AI's first draft is pasted whole, so the work is complete.","Opening the live URL and seeing the labeled dialogue plus your trace of accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions.","The HTML was long enough.","The AI said it looked good."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"One unedited draft is not a revision; the artifact is done when the live page carries the dialogue and the accept/modify/reject trace a reviewer can audit.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_one_draft_is_revision","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-02","cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-03","cat100-supplement-04-08-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_fluent_equals_correct","pattern":"fluent_equals_correct","description":"Treating confident, fluent AI teaching advice as accurate without checking it against evidence or developmental appropriateness","trigger":"learner accepts or publishes AI advice because it sounds authoritative, without verifying its claims","feedback":"Fluency is a style property, not an evidence check. Break the advice into claims and verify each one before it goes into the artifact or onto the page.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_persona_is_decoration","pattern":"persona_is_decoration","description":"Treating the teacher persona as flavor text rather than a constraint that shapes tone, audience, and what the AI may assert","trigger":"the persona block is present but the dialogue ignores its audience, knowledge bound, or hard stops","feedback":"The persona is the contract the dialogue must honor. Check each AI turn against the persona's audience, knowledge bound, and hard stops, not just its mood.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_8_one_draft_is_revision","pattern":"one_draft_is_revision","description":"Pasting the AI's first dialogue output as the finished artifact with no accepted, modified, and rejected trace","trigger":"the submitted artifact is a single unedited AI draft with no accept/modify/reject log","feedback":"One unedited draft shows no judgment. Keep the trace of what you accepted, modified, and rejected so a reviewer can audit the steering.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"10c30541b5a7598cd0a8060517220207edd0ca59610151767dbcba186813931c","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}